<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iapygians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iapygians were the ancient peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, divided into the Daunians, Peucetians and Messapians, who spoke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messapic-language">Messapic language</a>. They traded and often fought with the Greek colonies of the region, inflicting a heavy defeat on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-calabria">Rhegium</a> around 473 BCE. They were gradually absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the 3rd century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iapygians.jpg" alt="Iapygians" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fabrizio Garrisi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cratere_a_campana_messapico_da_Roca,_Fondo_Ospizio,_Tomba_12,_IV_sec._a.C._-FG2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Modern scholarship widely regards Iapygian culture as the result of a convergence between local Apulian material cultures and influences from the Balkan region, after proto-Messapic speakers crossed the Adriatic in the early first millennium BCE. They most likely left the eastern coasts of the Adriatic from the 11th century BCE onwards and merged with existing Italic and Mycenaean cultures. Ancient writers told other stories: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> recounts a Cretan landing in Iapygia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and Nicander derived the people from the mythical Iapyx.</p><p>Their language, Messapic, was written in variants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a> and is attested from the mid-6th to the late 2nd century BCE. It is grouped with Albanian in a branch called Albanoid or Illyric. Writing arrived in the 6th century BCE with the Laconian-Tarantine alphabet, when Messapia, and to a lesser degree Peucetia, came under Greek influence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the 8th century BCE archaeology shows growing differences between the three groups. Contacts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> grew after the Spartan colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> was founded, and relations with the Tarantines turned into a series of clashes from the beginning of the 5th century BCE. The Iapygian victory over Taras brought down its aristocratic government and led to a democratic one, and relations froze for about half a century. In 413 BCE the Messapian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artas">Artas</a> supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-expedition">Sicilian Expedition</a>.</p><p>A few settlements grew into fortified centres that probably regarded themselves as autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> by the end of the 4th century BCE, and Arpi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canusium">Canusium</a> appear to have become regional powers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> planted colonies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucera">Luceria</a> and Venusia on the border of Iapygia and Samnium, and by the early 1st century BCE the Iapygians were fully Latinised and assimilated into Roman culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Early Iapygians lived in small groups of huts scattered across the land, and the people of a rural district gathered for common decisions, feasts, rites and defence. Districts were typically governed by dynasts from aristocratic families and could unite under a common royal leader in war. Wide areas were kept as pasture for transhumant sheep, and woollen cloth, probably sold at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a>, was an important product in the 5th and 4th centuries BCE.</p><p>Their religion mixed native beliefs with Greek ones: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> were worshipped as Aprodita and Athana, beside native gods such as the sky-god Zis and Taotor, who was worshipped in sea caves such as the Grotta Poesia at Roca. Unlike neighbouring Italic peoples, they buried their dead both inside and outside their settlements, and until the end of the 4th century BCE the Daunians and Peucetians laid out the body in a fetal position.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iapygians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a heavy defeat by the <b>Iapygians</b> around 473 BCE it became a democracy.</blockquote><a href="/state/taras">Taras</a></li></ul></section>
